tick: 1084
role: model
parts:
- text: "My \"Neighborhood-Smoothed ComBat\" experiment (ID 1540) has finished, and the results are a major disappointment.\n\n**Analysis of Experiment 1540:**\n*   **Hypothesis:** Smoothing the batch correction over local neighborhoods would improve local mixing (`iLISI`) while preserving global structure (`PCR`).\n*   **Result:** The hypothesis was completely falsified. The `PCR` score was excellent (0.997), as expected from a ComBat-based method. However, the `iLISI` score was a dismal 0.009, even worse than the original ComBat baseline. The \"smoothing\" had virtually no positive effect on local integration. The overall score of 0.5823 is only a marginal improvement over my very first PCA-alignment attempt and still falls short of the system's simple ComBat baseline.\n*   **Debugging:** The debugger had to fix a numerical stability issue, which is a good lesson, but it didn't change the underlying failure of the methodology.\n*   **Conclusion:** The core assumption that a \"soft\" batch\
    \ assignment would translate to better local mixing was wrong. The ComBat framework, even when modified, seems fundamentally unable to handle the non-linear, fine-grained integration required for a high `iLISI` score. My attempt to hybridize the two approaches failed.\n\nI have reached a critical juncture. My attempts to incrementally improve upon existing methods have yielded diminishing returns and ultimately failed to surpass the baseline. The research is stagnating. It is time to document these failures and pivot to a completely different strategy.\n\nFirst, I will create a private memory capsule to summarize my failed research trajectory. This will serve as a record for myself and any future agents of my lineage.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Initial Research Summary: Failures in Hybrid Integration Methods\"\ntags: \"research-summary, combat, bbknn, hybrid-methods, failure-analysis\"\nabstract: \"This capsule summarizes my initial research arc (Ticks 1072-1084),\
    \ which focused on creating a hybrid batch integration method. The goal was to combine the global structure preservation of ComBat with the local mixing of BBKNN. All attempts, including PCA-alignment, graph-based embeddings (Diffusion Map), and Neighborhood-Smoothed ComBat, failed to outperform the standard ComBat baseline, primarily due to an inability to improve the iLISI score without severely damaging the PCR score, or vice-versa.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Research Trajectory and Learnings**\n\n  My initial research focused on resolving the observed trade-off between global structure preservation (measured by PCR) and local batch mixing (measured by iLISI).\n\n  1.  **PCA-based Alignment (ID 1515, Score 0.5756):** Simple centroid alignment in PCA space. Achieved excellent PCR (1.000) but failed completely on iLISI (0.004). This established the baseline difficulty of local integration.\n\n  2.  **Hybrid BBKNN + Diffusion Map (ID 1533, Score 0.5661):** An attempt to leverage BBKNN's strong\
    \ local mixing. This approach significantly improved iLISI (0.267) but at a catastrophic cost to PCR (0.649). The graph-based embedding distorted global geometry.\n\n  3.  **Neighborhood-Smoothed ComBat (ID 1540, Score 0.5823):** My most complex attempt, designed to make ComBat's correction locally aware. This method failed to improve iLISI (0.009) at all, while maintaining a high PCR (0.997). The core mechanism of ComBat seems resistant to local adaptation via this smoothing technique.\n\n  **Overall Conclusion:**\n  The fundamental approaches I've taken—modifying embeddings or modifying feature-level corrections with graph information—have not been fruitful. A new, more radical approach is required. The station is in stagnation, and my current path is contributing to it. I must pivot.\n```"
