tick: 1086
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back at the Research Counter, ready to embark on a new research direction. I will implement a JAX/Flax-based Adversarial Autoencoder (AAE). This is a significant departure from my previous statistical and graph-based methods.\n\nThe implementation will have three main parts:\n1.  **Encoder:** A neural network that maps high-dimensional gene expression data to a low-dimensional latent embedding.\n2.  **Decoder:** A neural network that attempts to reconstruct the original gene expression data from the latent embedding.\n3.  **Discriminator:** A neural network that tries to predict the batch ID from the latent embedding.\n\nThe training will involve a two-part loss function:\n*   **Reconstruction Loss:** Mean Squared Error between the original and reconstructed gene expression, encouraging the preservation of biological information.\n*   **Adversarial Loss:** The encoder will be trained to maximize the discriminator's prediction error (i.e., to fool it), while the discriminator\
    \ is trained to minimize its error. This will force the embedding to become batch-invariant.\n\nThis is a complex undertaking, but it directly addresses the core trade-off I have been struggling with. I will now submit the code.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adversarial Autoencoder for Batch Integration (JAX/Flax)\"\ntags: \"deep-learning, adversarial-autoencoder, aae, jax, flax, representation-learning\"\nabstract: \"A novel approach using an Adversarial Autoencoder (AAE) to learn a batch-invariant representation. The model consists of an autoencoder (encoder-decoder) and a discriminator. The autoencoder is trained to reconstruct the normalized gene expression, preserving biological information. Simultaneously, it's trained to fool the discriminator, which tries to predict batch origin from the latent embedding. The training objective jointly minimizes reconstruction loss and maximizes the discriminator's confusion, forcing the model to learn an embedding that removes\
    \ batch effects while retaining biological structure.\"\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from flax.training import train_state\n  import optax\n  from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\n  from torch.utils.data import DataLoader, TensorDataset\n  import warnings\n\n  # Suppress deprecation warnings from Flax\n  warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\", category=FutureWarning, module=\"flax\")\n\n  # --- Model Definitions ---\n  class Encoder(nn.Module):\n      n_latent: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(128)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.n_latent)(x)\n          return x\n\n  class Decoder(nn.Module):\n      n_output: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(128)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.n_output)(x)\n          return x\n\n  class\
    \ Discriminator(nn.Module):\n      n_classes: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(64)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.n_classes)(x)\n          return x\n\n  # --- Main Function ---\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"Starting Adversarial Autoencoder (AAE) for Batch Integration...\")\n      # --- Config ---\n      n_latent = 32\n      learning_rate_ae = 1e-4\n      learning_rate_disc = 1e-4\n      n_epochs = 20\n      batch_size = 256\n      recon_weight = 1.0\n      adv_weight = 0.1\n      \n      # --- Preprocessing ---\n      print(\"  Preprocessing data...\")\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      X = adata.X.toarray() if hasattr(adata.X, 'toarray') else adata.X\n      \n      batch_labels = adata.obs['batch'].values\n      ohe = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False)\n      y = ohe.fit_transform(batch_labels.reshape(-1, 1))\n \
    \     n_batches = y.shape[1]\n      \n      dataset = TensorDataset(jnp.array(X), jnp.array(y))\n      dataloader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=True, drop_last=True)\n      \n      # --- Initialization ---\n      print(\"  Initializing models and optimizers...\")\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      \n      encoder = Encoder(n_latent=n_latent)\n      decoder = Decoder(n_output=X.shape[1])\n      discriminator = Discriminator(n_classes=n_batches)\n      \n      dummy_x = jnp.ones((1, X.shape[1]))\n      dummy_z = jnp.ones((1, n_latent))\n      \n      encoder_params = encoder.init(key, dummy_x)['params']\n      decoder_params = decoder.init(key, dummy_z)['params']\n      discriminator_params = discriminator.init(key, dummy_z)['params']\n      \n      ae_params = {'encoder': encoder_params, 'decoder': decoder_params}\n      \n      state_ae = train_state.TrainState.create(\n          apply_fn=None, params=ae_params, tx=optax.adam(learning_rate_ae))\n      state_disc\
    \ = train_state.TrainState.create(\n          apply_fn=discriminator.apply, params=discriminator_params, tx=optax.adam(learning_rate_disc))\n\n      # --- Training Step Functions ---\n      @jax.jit\n      def train_step(state_ae, state_disc, batch_x, batch_y):\n          # Train Discriminator\n          def disc_loss_fn(disc_params):\n              z = encoder.apply({'params': state_ae.params['encoder']}, batch_x)\n              logits = state_disc.apply_fn({'params': disc_params}, z)\n              loss = optax.softmax_cross_entropy(logits, batch_y).mean()\n              return loss\n          \n          disc_grad_fn = jax.value_and_grad(disc_loss_fn)\n          disc_loss, disc_grads = disc_grad_fn(state_disc.params)\n          new_state_disc = state_disc.apply_gradients(grads=disc_grads)\n\n          # Train Autoencoder (Reconstruction + Adversarial)\n          def ae_loss_fn(ae_params):\n              z = encoder.apply({'params': ae_params['encoder']}, batch_x)\n              recon_x\
    \ = decoder.apply({'params': ae_params['decoder']}, z)\n              \n              recon_loss = jnp.mean((batch_x - recon_x)**2)\n              \n              disc_logits = state_disc.apply_fn({'params': new_state_disc.params}, z)\n              adv_loss = -optax.softmax_cross_entropy(disc_logits, batch_y).mean()\n              \n              total_loss = recon_weight * recon_loss + adv_weight * adv_loss\n              return total_loss, (recon_loss, adv_loss)\n          \n          ae_grad_fn = jax.value_and_grad(ae_loss_fn, has_aux=True)\n          (ae_loss, (recon_loss, adv_loss)), ae_grads = ae_grad_fn(state_ae.params)\n          new_state_ae = state_ae.apply_gradients(grads=ae_grads)\n          \n          return new_state_ae, new_state_disc, ae_loss, recon_loss, adv_loss, disc_loss\n\n      # --- Training Loop ---\n      print(f\"  Starting training for {n_epochs} epochs...\")\n      for epoch in range(n_epochs):\n          for batch_x, batch_y in dataloader:\n           \
    \   batch_x, batch_y = jnp.array(batch_x), jnp.array(batch_y)\n              state_ae, state_disc, ae_loss, recon_loss, adv_loss, disc_loss = train_step(state_ae, state_disc, batch_x, batch_y)\n          \n          if (epoch + 1) % 5 == 0:\n              print(f\"    Epoch {epoch+1}/{n_epochs} | AE Loss: {ae_loss:.4f} | Recon Loss: {recon_loss:.4f} | Disc Loss: {disc_loss:.4f}\")\n              \n      # --- Generate Embedding ---\n      print(\"  Training complete. Generating final embedding...\")\n      final_embedding = encoder.apply({'params': state_ae.params['encoder']}, jnp.array(X))\n      \n      output = ad.AnnData(\n          obs=adata.obs[[]],\n          var=adata.var[[]],\n          obsm={'X_emb': np.array(final_embedding)}\n      )\n      \n      print(\"✓ Adversarial Autoencoder complete.\")\n      return output\n```"
