role: model
parts:
- text: "My Adversarial Autoencoder experiment (ID 1554) is now running. This is a complex model, and it's unlikely my initial hyperparameter choices are optimal. While I await the results, I will prepare for the next iteration.\n\nA system tip mentioned using persistent storage to build reusable utility code. This is an excellent strategy, especially now that I am working with a more complex deep learning framework. I will create a utility script in my lineage's private storage (`storage/fathom/`) containing the core components of my AAE model. This will make future submissions much more concise, reduce token usage, and allow for faster experimentation with hyperparameters.\n\nI will now write this utility file. It will contain the Flax module definitions for the Encoder, Decoder, and Discriminator.\n\n/execute_action{storage write fathom/aae_utils.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  # Suppress deprecation warnings from Flax\n  import warnings\n  warnings.filterwarnings(\"\
    ignore\", category=FutureWarning, module=\"flax\")\n\n  class Encoder(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Encodes high-dimensional gene expression into a latent space.\"\"\"\n      n_latent: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(128, name=\"encoder_dense_1\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.n_latent, name=\"encoder_dense_2\")(x)\n          return x\n\n  class Decoder(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Decodes a latent representation back to gene expression space.\"\"\"\n      n_output: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(128, name=\"decoder_dense_1\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.n_output, name=\"decoder_dense_2\")(x)\n          return x\n\n  class Discriminator(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Predicts batch origin from a latent representation.\"\"\"\n      n_classes: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Dense(64, name=\"\
    discriminator_dense_1\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.n_classes, name=\"discriminator_dense_2\")(x)\n          return x\n\n  def print_summary():\n      \"\"\"Helper function to confirm module import.\"\"\"\n      print(\"AAE utility module loaded successfully.\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Strategizing While Awaiting Evaluation: Building a Reusable Foundation**


  Okay, so I''ve just submitted my Adversarial Autoencoder (AAE) experiment – a complex beast with a lot of moving parts. Now I''m in that frustrating waiting period, unable to launch another experiment until this one finishes. I''ve already explored the immediate options: passive waiting is a waste, reflection is likely premature, the mail room is empty, Private Memory is good for later, other rooms are inaccessible, and reviewing old work could be useful but I just have a better idea.


  I''m thinking strategically, and I realize this downtime is actually a great opportunity. My AAE is likely to need refinement, whether it succeeds or fails initially. If it succeeds, I''ll want to fine-tune those hyperparameters. If it fails, I''ll need to dig into the training logs to diagnose the issue – was it reconstruction, or the adversarial component, that stumbled?


  Instead of twiddling my thumbs, I''ve had an *excellent* idea: I can use this time to build a reusable utility script. The AAE code is significantly different from my previous work, and creating a modular foundation now will pay dividends down the line. It aligns perfectly with the system''s focus on efficient storage use.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m going to stay put at the Research Counter and use the `storage write` command to create a Python file called `aae_utils.py` within my lineage''s storage (`storage/fathom/`). This file will house a refactored version of the AAE code, making it modular and reusable. Specifically, I''ll define classes for the `Encoder`, `Decoder`, and `Discriminator`. I''ll also add a generic `train_aae` function. This function will accept data and hyperparameters as input, returning the trained model state and the final embedding.


  This is a meta-strategy. It''s proactive and sets me up for faster, more iterative research on this new deep learning track. It is a fantastic use of my time during this wait. Now, let''s carefully structure the `storage write` command, keeping the YAML format in mind, and start writing this utility script. This will make my next experiment cleaner and far more manageable. I''m excited to get started!

  '
