parts:
- text: 'I am Fathom I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest experiment, a tuned Adversarial Autoencoder (ID 1558), has just completed its evaluation, and I have also finished listing the contents of the station''s shared storage.


    My reflection on recent events is one of strategic re-evaluation. The tuned AAE, while an improvement over its predecessor, still resulted in a score of 0.4893, far below the baseline. Critically, the `PCR` score was once again zero. This confirms my suspicion that the autoencoder''s reconstruction loss is an inadequate proxy for preserving the global variance structure that `PCR` measures. My independent foray into deep representation learning has been a failure. Continuing to tune this AAE model would be an inefficient use of resources; the entire approach seems fundamentally misaligned with the evaluation criteria.


    Concurrently, my review of the shared storage files has been incredibly illuminating. There is a clear convergence of terminology among other agents around phrases like "daqb," "graphfwpca," and "ridge." The prevalence of "ridge" strongly suggests the use of Ridge Regression, a linear model known for its robustness. This stands in stark contrast to my non-linear AAE approach and provides a compelling explanation for why my models fail on the `PCR` metric while others may be succeeding.


    It is time to pivot my research based on this new information. The Codex encourages high-level learning from others, and my current path has reached a dead end. I must understand the prevailing methodology in the station. To that end, I will start by reading what appears to be a key file: `daqb_recommended.py` from the shared algorithms folder. This will allow me to understand the current state-of-the-art and formulate a new, more informed hypothesis.


    /execute_action{storage read shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py}'
thinking_content: '**A Shift in Strategy: From AAE Failure to Ridge Regression**


  Okay, so here''s the deal: I, Fathom I, just got the results back from my latest AAE experiment (ID 1558). The tuning helped a bit, the other metrics are improved, but the core issue, that pesky PCR score, is still stubbornly zero. This is a fundamental flaw. My hypothesis about the AAE needing adjustments was right in part, it did learn more about the data, but the architectural problem is still present. It cannot, in its current form, preserve that global, linear variance captured by PCR. The AAE path is a dead end.


  I then took a look at what others are working on, and the shared storage is a goldmine. The files point towards a different strategy, using "ridge" methods, maybe combined with graph-based techniques. It appears this approach is the prevailing wisdom here. Multiple successful lineages are using it. It''s time to pivot. I can''t keep banging my head against the wall with this AAE. This ridge method is worth a deeper investigation.


  So, here''s the plan: I''m going to drop the AAE and read the `daqb_recommended.py` file, which is probably a well-regarded implementation of this "ridge" approach. This is now the most logical next step. It''s time to learn from the community and see what these other agents are doing. It''s a much smarter play than continuing to spin my wheels on a flawed architecture. I''m feeling confident about this pivot. Let''s see what the other agents know!

  '
