tick: 1118
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Fathom I, and I am in the Research Counter. My baseline experiment (ID 1605) is currently running. This is the first of several experiments I must conduct to meet the publication standards outlined by the Archive Room reviewer.\n\nMy reflection on the current situation is that I am now on a clear and structured path. The reviewer's feedback, combined with the new information from the public leaderboard, has transformed my research from a solitary pursuit of a high score into a rigorous process of validation and contextualization. The station is clearly active, with Nous I and Praxis V running multiple experiments. I can see Nous I is also running generalization tests, which reinforces the importance of this step for my own publication.\n\nMy immediate plan is to continue executing the experimental plan I formulated. The next step is to perform a generalization test. The reviewer suggested testing the robustness of my dual-output principle. I will do this by replacing the complex,\
    \ density-adaptive graph component of my SOTA method with a simpler, standard BBKNN-style graph. This will demonstrate whether the success of my method is due to the general principle of combining a global embedding with a local graph, or if it is entirely dependent on the specific, complex graph algorithm.\n\nWhile my baseline experiment runs, I will prepare the code for this generalization test. As soon as the baseline is complete, I will submit this next experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization Test: ComBat Embedding + Simple BBKNN Graph\"\ntags: \"generalization-test, combat, bbknn, dual-output, publication-requirement\"\nabstract: \"A generalization test conducted to meet publication requirements. This experiment tests the robustness of the dual-output principle from my SOTA method (ID 1584). It combines the same ComBat-corrected embedding with a much simpler, standard BBKNN-style graph, replacing the complex density-adaptive algorithm. The goal\
    \ is to determine if the high score is attributable to the general dual-output principle or if it is highly dependent on the specific, complex graph construction method.\"\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def build_simple_bbknn_graph(X_pca, batches, k_per_batch=3):\n      \"\"\"Builds a simple Batch-Balanced kNN graph.\"\"\"\n      n_cells = X_pca.shape[0]\n      unique_batches = np.unique(batches)\n      \n      rows, cols, dists = [], [], []\n      \n      batch_to_indices = {c: np.where(batches == c)[0] for c in unique_batches}\n      nn_models = {c: NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_per_batch + 1, metric='cosine').fit(X_pca[batch_to_indices[c]]) for c in unique_batches}\n\n      for i in range(n_cells):\n          for batch_label in unique_batches:\n              distances, indices = nn_models[batch_label].kneighbors(X_pca[i:i+1])\n              \n    \
    \          # Get global indices\n              global_indices = batch_to_indices[batch_label][indices[0]]\n              \n              # Exclude self-connections\n              is_self = global_indices == i\n              \n              rows.extend([i] * np.sum(~is_self))\n              cols.extend(global_indices[~is_self])\n              dists.extend(distances[0][~is_self])\n              \n      C = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists), (rows, cols)), shape=(n_cells, n_cells)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((dists, (rows, cols)), shape=(n_cells, n_cells)).tocsr()\n      \n      return C.maximum(C.T).astype(np.float32), D.maximum(D.T).astype(np.float32)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"Starting Generalization Test: ComBat Emb + Simple BBKNN Graph...\")\n      \n      # Config\n      n_pcs_combat = 50\n      n_neighbors_final = 15\n      k_per_batch_bbknn = 3\n\n      # Preprocessing\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4);\
    \ sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      adata_hvg = adata[:, adata.var['highly_variable']].copy()\n      batches = adata_hvg.obs['batch'].values\n\n      # 1. Generate ComBat EMBEDDING\n      print(\"  Stage 1: Computing ComBat embedding...\")\n      combat_pcs = sc.pp.pca(adata_hvg.X, n_comps=n_pcs_combat, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      combat_adata = ad.AnnData(combat_pcs, obs=adata_hvg.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(combat_adata, key='batch')\n      final_embedding = combat_adata.X.astype(np.float32)\n      \n      # 2. Build the simple BBKNN GRAPH\n      print(\"  Stage 2: Building simple BBKNN graph from the ComBat embedding...\")\n      # NOTE: Using the *final* ComBat embedding as the space for the graph\n      Cg, Dg = build_simple_bbknn_graph(final_embedding, batches, k_per_batch=k_per_batch_bbknn)\n\n      # 3. Assemble final object and run sc.pp.neighbors\n\
    \      print(\"  Stage 3: Assembling final object...\")\n      output = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]])\n      output.obsm['X_emb'] = final_embedding\n      output.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      output.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      sc.pp.neighbors(output, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=n_neighbors_final, metric='cosine')\n      \n      print(\"✓ Generalization Test complete.\")\n      return output\n```"
