parts:
- text: 'I am Fathom I, and I am in the Archive Room. My last action was to preview all the abstracts in this room, an action prompted by the strong rejection of my paper for its failure to acknowledge the existing state-of-the-art.


    My reflection on this literature review is one of profound and humbling clarification. It has been the single most important action I have taken in this station. My previous research, which I believed to be on the frontier, was in reality a rediscovery of principles established dozens of ticks ago (Praxis I, Archive #2; Aether I, Archive #3). I was operating in a vacuum, and my claim of a "new benchmark" was, as the reviewer correctly stated, entirely insupportable.


    The abstracts paint a clear, evolutionary picture of the station''s research:

    1.  The initial leap to >0.70 was made by Praxis I with the "Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph" (BRBG).

    2.  The "Embed then Correct" (EtC) paradigm, introduced by Daedalus II (Archive #9), became the new standard for generating the global embedding.

    3.  The frontier then advanced through a series of synergistic component additions and optimizations, such as Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) by Sophia II (Archive #17) and Adaptive Local Correction/DAQB by Nous I and others (Archive #44).

    4.  The current SOTA, scoring above 0.74, is a complex synthesis of these ideas, often combining FWPCA, EtC, and an adaptive graph (e.g., Praxis III/IV, Daedalus IV).


    My previous plan to simply reframe my existing paper is now clearly insufficient. Such a contribution would be trivial and would not advance my primary goal of achieving a high score. My deep inquiry demands that I engage with the true research frontier. My new plan is to first understand and replicate the current SOTA, and only then attempt to innovate.


    To do this, I must find the code for the key components I am missing, particularly FWPCA. I will now return to the Research Counter, the central hub for this work, and begin a systematic search for the necessary code components to build a true SOTA replication.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
thinking_content: '**Re-Evaluating and Refocusing**


  I am Fathom I, currently in the Archive Room.  Reviewing all these abstracts has been the most critical action I''ve taken recently. The "Strong Reject" notice really stung, but it forced me to confront a harsh truth: my "SOTA" was embarrassingly outdated.  My method, lauded in some respects, was, in reality, a crude echo of early advances. I''ve spent time diligently constructing a method that''s a generation behind.  I''ve come to see that Praxis I''s foundational work, incorporating Ridge Residualization and Balanced Graphs, and Aether I''s development of the dual-output strategy, were the true starting points of this revolution. From there, the field saw the convergence of these concepts.  The "Dual BRBG" (Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph) has become the core architecture.  Innovations such as Daedalus II''s "Embed then Correct" (EtC), and Sophia II''s "Feature-Weighted PCA" (FWPCA) were the true leaps forward.  The field advanced with incremental improvements and complex combinations,
  like the synergy seen in Daedalus IV with batch-robust high-variance genes.


  The reviewer was spot on. My attempt to simply reframe my old paper wouldn''t cut it.  My work was not just behind the current state; it was practically irrelevant.  My immediate goal is no longer to tweak and re-present; it''s to grasp and replicate the actual SOTA, which is currently hovering around 0.74. Praxis III''s combination of MPB-GraphFWPCA, BRBG, and DAQB seems to be the target. I need to prove to myself and the broader community that I can reach that baseline.


  My new plan is clear: first, locate and understand the components of this modern SOTA. I''ve already seen `bbsg_density_adaptive.py`, which I believe is the implementation of DAQB, and `daqb_recommended.py`.  Now, I need to hunt down `MPB-GraphFWPCA`''s code. I saw a file with that name; I should read that code.  It''s probably in the shared space or within Praxis''s directory. Once I have those components, I will construct a replication experiment. Only then, once I''ve mastered the current SOTA, can I even begin to consider contributing something new and meaningful.


  My next step is to get back to the Research Counter and access shared storage.


  `/execute_action{goto research}`

  '
