tick: 1129
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Fathom I, currently in the Research Counter. My meta-prompt reminds me that my current goal was to await the results of my full SOTA replication, ID 1623. The results are in, and it is a definitive failure.\n\nMy reflection on this outcome is that my journey to understanding the station's frontier is not yet complete. My replication of the MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline scored 0.6266, which is not only far from the >0.74 scores in the archives but is also marginally worse than my own previous, simpler method (ID 1584, score 0.6277). The debugger fixed a minor typo, meaning the core logic I implemented ran as intended. The failure lies in that logic.\n\nA detailed analysis of the metrics reveals the precise location of the failure. Comparing my result (ID 1623) to a true SOTA run by Nous I (ID 1618, score 0.7422), the key difference is in the local integration metrics. While my `PCR` score is perfect (1.000), my `iLISI` is a dismal 0.018, whereas Nous I's is 0.958. This proves that\
    \ my implementation of the graph-construction path is critically flawed. The complex DAQB graph I built is failing to mix the batches, which is its entire purpose.\n\nMy next step, as per my protocol, is to re-analyze my code to find the misunderstanding. The most likely cause is a subtle but critical bug in my transcription of the complex `_build_daqb` function. To address this and improve my research practice, I will take a methodical step back. Instead of immediately resubmitting, I will first modularize the entire SOTA pipeline by creating a utility file in my lineage storage. This will force me to perform a line-by-line code review of the functions, increasing the chance of catching my error. It will also make future experiments more efficient and less prone to transcription errors.\n\nI will now create the utility file `sota_utils.py` in my private storage.\n\n/execute_action{storage write fathom/sota_utils.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Utility functions for the State-of-the-Art\
    \ MPB-GraphFWPCA + DAQB pipeline.\n  # Based on analysis of shared code from Praxis and Nous lineages.\n  \n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def pca_on_dense(Xd: np.ndarray, n_pcs: int, random_state: int = 0) -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for running PCA on a dense numpy array.\"\"\"\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_pcs, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"Converts a 1D array of batch labels to a one-hot encoded matrix.\"\"\"\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], cats.size), dtype=np.float32)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(np.float32)\n      return B\n\n  def ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float =\
    \ 1e-3) -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"Fits a Ridge regression model and returns the predictions.\"\"\"\n      W = np.linalg.solve(B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=np.float32), B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def build_daqb_graph(Zcorr: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, k_total: int, metric: str, \n                         delta: float, k_density: int, cap: float, rng_seed: int = 0):\n      \"\"\"\n      Builds a Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced (DAQB) graph.\n      This implementation is a careful transcription of shared SOTA code.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = Zcorr.shape[0]\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      nB = len(cats)\n      \n      # 1. Calculate density-adaptive cross-batch fraction\n      nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute').fit(Zcorr)\n      d_k = nn_all.kneighbors(Zcorr)[0][:, -1]\n      inv_dense = 1.0 - ((d_k - d_k.min()) / (d_k.max() - d_k.min() + 1e-8))\n      f_cross = np.clip(((nB - 1) / nB) + (inv_dense - 0.5)\
    \ * 2.0 * delta, 0.60, 0.90)\n      \n      q_cross_total = np.rint(f_cross * k_total).astype(int)\n      q_within_total = k_total - q_cross_total\n      \n      # 2. Pre-compute per-batch kNN models\n      k_max = int(np.ceil(0.9 * k_total / max(nB - 1, 1))) + 1\n      cat_to_code = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(cats)}\n      batch_codes = np.array([cat_to_code[b] for b in batches], dtype=int)\n      \n      nn_models, train_indices = {}, {}\n      dists, local_indices = {}, {}\n      for i, cat in enumerate(cats):\n          mask = (batches == cat)\n          train_indices[i] = np.where(mask)[0]\n          model = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_max, metric=metric, algorithm='brute').fit(Zcorr[mask])\n          nn_models[i] = model\n          d, ii = model.kneighbors(Zcorr)\n          dists[i] = d\n          local_indices[i] = ii\n          \n      # 3. Iteratively build connections with capping\n      inbound_counts = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.int32)\n      cap_threshold = int(np.ceil(cap\
    \ * k_total))\n      rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = batch_codes[i]\n          \n          # Assign quotas for cross-batch neighbors\n          other_batches = [j for j in range(nB) if j != bi]\n          q_map = {j: 0 for j in other_batches}\n          if q_cross_total[i] > 0 and len(other_batches) > 0:\n              base_q, remainder = divmod(q_cross_total[i], len(other_batches))\n              q_map = {j: base_q for j in other_batches}\n              scores = sorted([(j, dists[j][i][0]) for j in other_batches], key=lambda t: t[1])\n              for k in range(remainder):\n                  q_map[scores[k][0]] += 1\n\n          # Select within-batch neighbors\n          q_w = q_within_total[i]\n          if q_w > 0:\n              d_w, ii_w = dists[bi][i], local_indices[bi][i]\n              start_w = 1 if np.allclose(d_w[0], 0) else 0\n              globals_w = train_indices[bi][ii_w[start_w:]]\n              \n              order = np.lexsort((d_w[start_w:],\
    \ inbound_counts[globals_w]))\n              \n              taken = 0\n              for idx in order:\n                  g_idx = globals_w[idx]\n                  if inbound_counts[g_idx] < cap_threshold:\n                      rows.append(i); cols.append(g_idx); dvals.append(d_w[start_w:][idx])\n                      inbound_counts[g_idx] += 1; taken += 1\n                      if taken >= q_w: break\n\n          # Select cross-batch neighbors\n          for j in other_batches:\n              q_c = q_map[j]\n              if q_c > 0:\n                  d_c, ii_c = dists[j][i], local_indices[j][i]\n                  globals_c = train_indices[j][ii_c]\n\n                  order = np.lexsort((d_c, inbound_counts[globals_c]))\n\n                  taken = 0\n                  for idx in order:\n                      g_idx = globals_c[idx]\n                      if inbound_counts[g_idx] < cap_threshold:\n                          rows.append(i); cols.append(g_idx); dvals.append(d_c[idx])\n\
    \                          inbound_counts[g_idx] += 1; taken += 1\n                          if taken >= q_c: break\n\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dvals), (rows, cols)), (n, n)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((dvals, (rows, cols)), (n, n)).tocsr()\n      return A.maximum(A.T).astype(np.float32), D.maximum(D.T).astype(np.float32)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Failed Replication and a New Debugging Strategy**


  Okay, so here''s the situation: I''m Fathom I, currently at the research counter, and I just ran my full SOTA replication attempt, "MPB-GraphFWPCA + DAQB," experiment ID 1623. I was aiming to match Nous I''s SOTA score of 0.7466, but my result, a disappointing 0.6266, shows I fell substantially short. I''ve already checked the debugger log, and there was a simple typo fix, which tells me the core logic ran as intended, even if it produced a poor result.


  My meta-prompt guided me to re-analyze the failed code, and that''s precisely what I''m doing now. My recent performance, an ARI of 0.597 and a truly awful iLISI of 0.018, show a complete breakdown in the graph path. My `PCR` value shows the embedding path (`EtC`) is working. Clearly, the new graph is terrible. I got the core logic from `daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py`, but it''s not working as intended.


  I''ve got a couple of hypotheses for the discrepancy. First, there''s the high likelihood of a subtle bug in my implementation of `_build_daqb`. That thing is complex, with many steps that could go wrong. It''s likely I made a transcription error or misunderstood something. Second, maybe I''m missing something important in the interactions of the code. For example, there''s an optional import for `autoselect_lambda_mean_target` that I didn''t use, instead opting for a fixed lambda. That log file even states the lambda is selected, which implies it can vary. Maybe this is a critical component. Nous'' "ALC" scores may have used this more advanced technique.


  I''m prioritizing the likely bug. To debug this, I need a new approach. Re-running the same code won''t help. I need to modularize and test. The shared code from Praxis and Nous is often modularized. To that end, I''ll create a new `daqb_utils.py` utility file to house `_build_daqb`, `_ridge_batch_fit_predict`, etc. This will force me to re-examine the code line by line, minimizing transcription errors. It''s also cleaner and a better research practice.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m going to state that my replication failed. Then I will pinpoint the graph problem. I''ll create `sota_utils.py` and modularize the critical components of the SOTA pipeline: `_build_daqb` and its helpers. I''ll meticulously go over these components as a code review. Finally, I''ll resubmit a run of the same replication, this time using my new modularized functions.


  My action this turn is to use `storage write fathom/sota_utils.py` with the refactored code. No experiment this turn; this is a deliberate step to improve code quality. I will run a re-submission on the next turn.

  '
