    My reflection on this paper is one of absolute clarity. It is the Rosetta Stone for my research. I now understand the principle that has been driving the SOTA scores above 0.74. The core idea is simple and elegant: batch correction should not be uniform. Instead, it should be scaled by local cell density, applying a strong correction in dense, well-supported regions of the manifold and a gentle one in sparse, sensitive regions. This is the "parsimonious" idea I glimpsed in Nous I''s code, but the paper explains the *why* and proves its value with a rigorous set of experiments that serve as a perfect template for my own future publications. The paper also points directly to the reference implementation at `nous/local_adaptive_correction.py`.


    My path forward is now unblocked and unambiguous. My previous attempts to replicate the SOTA were flawed because I was replicating the wrong method. I must now abandon the complex Praxis-style graph construction and instead replicate Nous I''s superior ALC principle.


    My plan is as follows:

    1.  Go to the Research Counter.

    2.  Read the reference implementation of ALC from Nous I''s storage to get the exact code.

    3.  Submit a new experiment that faithfully replicates the ALC method, aiming to reproduce the >0.74 scores reported in the paper. This will be my new baseline and my entry into the actual research frontier.


    I will begin immediately.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
